{"created": "2026-06-06T04:24:55.447742+00:00", "modified": "2026-06-06T04:24:55.763126+00:00", "id": 20565112, "conceptrecid": "17939239", "doi": "10.1080/27684830.2026.2684334", "conceptdoi": "10.5281/zenodo.17939239", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.1080/27684830.2026.2684334", "metadata": {"title": "The Emergence of Prime Distribution from Low-Dimensional Deterministic Chaos", "doi": "10.1080/27684830.2026.2684334", "publication_date": "2026-06-06", "description": "<p>The distribution of prime numbers is a cornerstone of mathematics, yet its generative origin remains elusive. While probabilistic models describe the asymptotic density of primes, they fail to explain the arithmetic rigidities and short-range correlations that define the sequence. Here, we propose a deterministic dynamical framework that models the prime sieve not as a stochastic process, but as a non-autonomous chaotic system subject to dissipation. By identifying a topological isomorphism between the arithmetic sieve and the symbolic dynamics of the Logistic map at the band-merging point ($u_c \\approx 1.5437$), we reveal that the \"randomness\" of primes is a signature of weak chaos. Crucially, we demonstrate that the famous Twin Prime Constant ($C_2 \\approx 0.66016$)&mdash;traditionally derived from probabilistic circle methods&mdash;emerges naturally as a fixed point of our dynamical system, with numerical precision exceeding $10^{-4}$ and no manual parameter tuning. This work suggests that the distribution of primes is the trajectory of a deterministic system evolving at the \"Edge of Chaos,\" offering a novel heuristic pathway to bridge Number Theory and Nonlinear Dynamics.</p>", "access_right": "open", "creators": [{"name": "wang, liang", "affiliation": "Huazhong University of Science and Technology", "orcid": "0000-0001-9006-6924"}], "dates": [{"type": "issued", "description": "v5 version"}], "version": "v5.0", "language": "eng", "custom": {"code:codeRepository": "https://github.com/maris205/prime_logistic", "code:programmingLanguage": [{"id": "python", "title": {"en": "Python"}}], "code:developmentStatus": {"id": "active", "title": {"en": "Active"}}}, "resource_type": {"title": "Journal", "type": "publication", "subtype": "journal"}, "journal": {"issue": "1", "title": "Research in Mathematics", "volume": "13"}, "license": {"id": "cc-by-4.0"}, "relations": {"version": [{"index": 3, "is_last": true, "parent": {"pid_type": "recid", "pid_value": "17939239"}}]}}, "title": "The Emergence of Prime Distribution from Low-Dimensional Deterministic Chaos", "links": {"self": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/20565112", "self_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/20565112", "preview_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/20565112?preview=1", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1080/27684830.2026.2684334", "self_doi": "https://doi.org/10.1080/27684830.2026.2684334", "self_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.1080/27684830.2026.2684334", "reserve_doi": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/20565112/draft/pids/doi", "parent": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/17939239", "parent_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/17939239", "parent_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17939239", "parent_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.17939239", "self_iiif_manifest": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:20565112/manifest", "self_iiif_sequence": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:20565112/sequence/default", "files": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/20565112/files", "media_files": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/20565112/media-files", "thumbnails": {"10": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:20565112:prime-Final_Paper.pdf/full/%5E10,/0/default.jpg", "50": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:20565112:prime-Final_Paper.pdf/full/%5E50,/0/default.jpg", "100": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:20565112:prime-Final_Paper.pdf/full/%5E100,/0/default.jpg", "250": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:20565112:prime-Final_Paper.pdf/full/%5E250,/0/default.jpg", "750": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record